 dict(pmid='43016216', doi='10.53738/revmed.2026.22.983.49248', journal='Rev Med Suisse', title='[Asthma, air pollution and climate change].', short='Neuchatel Geneva Rev Med Suisse narrative review asthma air pollution climate change pollen thunderstorm wildfire', design='Narrative review', pm='Outdoor and indoor air pollution (literature)', geo='Switzerland (clinical audience)', endpoint='Asthma development and control', tier='C', basis='Review / no primary exposure', affil='Reseau hospitalier neuchatelois, Switzerland; Hopitaux universitaires de Geneve, Switzerland', n='Short French-language clinical review for Swiss practitioners. Outdoor and indoor pollution are described as major contributors to poor asthma control and possibly to disease onset; climate change adds longer pollen seasons, thunderstorm asthma, wildfires and flooding. Individual protective measures are judged of limited effectiveness, and source reduction through transport, urban planning and energy policy the most effective strategy, with a role for clinicians in education and advocacy. No new data, no stated search, and the effectiveness judgement on individual measures is not referenced in the abstract. Relevance: indirect - the scepticism about personal measures is relevant to sensor-driven personal alerting, which is where that judgement would need testing.', sub=corpus.RESP),
